$article=['date'=>'2026-10-13','slug'=>'behaviour-or-sensory-issue','tag'=>'Occupational Therapy','category'=>'Occupational Therapy & Sensory Processing','breadcrumb'=>'Behaviour or Sensory Issue?','meta_title'=>'Sensory Processing Disorder Signs or Difficult Behaviour?','description'=>'Learn common sensory processing disorder signs, how overload can resemble difficult behaviour, and what parents can observe before seeking occupational therapy.','h1'=>'Is It a Behaviour Problem or a Sensory Issue? A Parent&rsquo;s Guide','dek'=>'Instead of asking whether the child meant it, first ask what their nervous system and the situation were demanding.','body'=><<<'HTML'
<p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light">Sensory processing disorder signs can look like refusal, defiance or attention-seeking. A child may flee a noisy room, refuse clothing, chew objects or crash into furniture because sensory input feels overwhelming or because they are seeking more input to feel organised. Behaviour and sensory processing are not competing explanations, however. Behaviour always has context and may serve several functions.</p>
<h2>What sensory processing means</h2><p>Sensory processing is how the nervous system notices, sorts and responds to information from sight, sound, touch, taste, smell, movement, body position and internal sensations. Children can be sensitive to some input and seek other input.</p><p>A strong preference is not automatically a disorder. Concern grows when sensory responses repeatedly restrict dressing, eating, sleep, school, play or family life.</p>
<h2>Look at what happens before the behaviour</h2><p>“He screamed” describes the visible event but not its function. Note the room, noise, clothing, hunger, fatigue, transition, instruction and social demand immediately before it. Then note what changed afterwards.</p><p>If leaving a noisy room consistently brings relief, overload may be involved. If the same behaviour appears when any limit is set and ends when a desired object is provided, goal-directed protest may contribute. Both can occur in the same child.</p>
<h2>Common sensory patterns</h2><ul><li><strong>Sound:</strong> covering ears, fleeing hand dryers, making repetitive noise or turning volume high.</li><li><strong>Touch:</strong> distress with seams, hair care or light touch, or seeking strong hugs and pressure.</li><li><strong>Movement:</strong> avoiding swings and stairs, or constantly spinning, jumping and crashing.</li><li><strong>Oral input:</strong> gagging at textures, chewing clothing or putting non-food objects in the mouth.</li><li><strong>Visual input:</strong> distress in cluttered, bright spaces or fascination with lights and movement.</li></ul>
<h2>Keep a one-week pattern log</h2><p>Record when and where an event occurred, the sensory and social demands, early signs, what the child did and what helped. Patterns across several examples are more informative than one difficult day.</p>
<h2>Support first, teach when regulated</h2><p>During overload, reduce input and language, protect safety and offer a familiar calm space. After recovery, teach communication, boundaries and coping. Accommodation does not mean removing every expectation. It means making participation possible before expecting learning.</p>
<p>Avoid generic “sensory diets” copied online. The American Occupational Therapy Association recommends documented assessment before sensory-based intervention because children with similar behaviour may need different support.</p>
<div class="safety-card"><p><strong>Safety comes first:</strong> sudden changes, pain, self-injury, eating risks or loss of skills require appropriate medical or developmental review, not a sensory explanation alone.</p></div>
<h2>What an occupational therapist examines</h2><p>An OT considers sensory patterns in relation to real activities, such as dressing, classroom participation and play. The goal is not to make a child tolerate everything. It is to improve safe, meaningful participation. Learn about <a href="/occupational-therapy/sensory-processing-disorder">sensory processing assessment and support</a>.</p>
