'faqs'=>[['Are sensory issues always part of autism?','No. Sensory differences can occur with several developmental profiles or without an autism diagnosis. Autism assessment considers social communication and restricted or repetitive patterns too.'],['Can a child be both sensitive and sensory-seeking?','Yes. A child may avoid particular sounds while seeking movement or pressure. Responses can also change with fatigue, illness, stress and the surrounding environment.'],['Does accommodating sensory needs reinforce bad behaviour?','A suitable accommodation reduces an access barrier. Boundaries and skills can still be taught, but children learn them more effectively when they are regulated and safe.'],['When should we see an occupational therapist?','Consider assessment when sensory responses repeatedly interfere with dressing, eating, sleep, learning, play or community activities despite reasonable adjustments at home or school.']],
